\title[The Law Is Universal, the Conductor Is Not]{The Law Is Universal, the Conductor Is Not:\\ One-Bit Sign Channels in $S_3$ Cubic Fields}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{September 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $K$ be the splitting field of an irreducible rational cubic with Galois group $S_3$. For each unramified prime $p$, the \emph{splitting type} of the cubic modulo $p$ (three roots, one root, or none) is the cycle type of the Frobenius permutation. We study how much information the splitting type carries about the sign character of Frobenius, and where that character can be read off from $p$ itself.

Our first result is group-theoretic and fully general. For every finite group $G$ with the uniform measure, every surjective character $\chi\colon G\to\{\pm1\}$, and every type function $T$ on $G$ through which $\chi$ factors, the mutual information is exactly one bit: $I(\chi;T)=1$. No type function can carry more than one bit about $\chi$. The same holds for the product character on $G\times G$ (the ``semiprime pair channel''), and a function of one coordinate of a product carries zero information about a function of the other. Since the shape of the channel depends only on the group, it is the same for every $S_3$-field.

Our second group of results is arithmetic. We give an elementary proof of Stickelberger's parity law for depressed cubics $x^3+ax+b$ over $\F_p$ ($p$ odd, $\Delta=-4a^3-27b^2\ne0$): the cubic has exactly one root in $\F_p$ if and only if $\Delta$ is a non-square. We then compare $x^3-2$ ($\Delta=-108=-3\cdot6^2$) with $x^3+x+1$ ($\Delta=-31$). For $x^3-2$ and every prime $p>3$, there is exactly one root iff $p\equiv2\pmod3$. For $x^3+x+1$ and every prime $p\notin\{2,31\}$, there is exactly one root iff $\leg{p}{31}=-1$. The primes $5\equiv11\pmod 3$ show that the sign bit of $x^3+x+1$ is not a function of $p\bmod3$. \emph{Corrected verdict:} the one-bit law is universal in the shape of the channel, which is dictated by the group. The conductor at which the bit can be read is dictated by the discriminant: $3$ for $x^3-2$, $31$ for $x^3+x+1$.
\end{abstract}

